{
  "slug": "artist:queen-latifah",
  "name": "Queen Latifah",
  "editorial_article": "Born Dana Elaine Owens in 1970, Queen Latifah established her foundation in the late 1980s hip-hop scene, eventually earning the title Queen of Jazz Rap. Her third studio album, 1993's Black Reign, achieved gold certification in the United States and peaked at number 15 on the US R&B charts. Throughout her career, she has transitioned between rap-focused projects like Nature of a Sista' and jazz-inflected releases such as 2004's The Dana Owens Album and 2007's Trav'lin' Light. She has maintained a prolific collaborative history, appearing on tracks ranging from the 1994 remix of Brandy's I Wanna Be Down to a 2011 Grammy-winning arrangement of Who Can I Turn To (When Nobody Needs Me) with Tony Bennett. Her work has appeared on numerous soundtracks, including New Jack City, Chicago, and Hairspray, while her 2009 Persona album was released through Flavor Unit and Universal Records.",
